//
// Closes "forgotten" open opportunities whose CloseDate has passed.
// Called synchronously by a Schedulable job; returns the number of
// opportunities that were moved to 'Closed Lost'.
//
// Notes for the reviewer (why the code looks the way it does, and what
// I would consider changing):
//
//  1. The filter uses IsClosed = false rather than checking a list of
//     stage names. IsClosed is maintained by Salesforce for every stage
//     (including custom and Closed Lost/Won variants), so it stays
//     correct even if stages are added or renamed later. Opportunities
//     whose CloseDate equals the cutoff stay open, per the requirement.
//
//  2. The list-form SOQL for loop retrieves 200 rows per iteration and
//     each chunk is updated immediately, so heap usage stays flat
//     regardless of volume (a few thousand rows is well within both
//     the 10k DML-row limit and synchronous CPU limits when driven
//     from a Schedulable's execute()).
//
//  3. Things I would change or watch out for:
//     a. DML inside the SOQL for loop works, but if a single record
//        fails validation the whole `update` throws and nothing in
//        that chunk (and nothing after it) is closed. If partial
//        success is acceptable, switch to
//        Database.update(chunk, false) and count successes from the
//        returned SaveResult list so the reported count stays honest.
//     b. If volumes grow towards tens of thousands of rows per run,
//        move this into a Batch Apex (or Database.Batchable +
//        schedulable chaining) so the whole run is not one giant
//        transaction — that also isolates each chunk's failures.
//     c. The Schedulable's running user matters: this runs in system
//        mode, so row sharing is bypassed. That is appropriate for a
//        system cleanup job, but it means no user-level filtering is
//        applied. If that ever needs to change, pass a running user
//        or scope the query explicitly.
//     d. Consider writing an audit record (or at least a debug log)
//        per run summarizing what was closed, so "the nightly job did
//        nothing" is distinguishable from "it failed silently".
//
//     None of these block the current requirement, so the class below
//     implements exactly the behavior requested.

public with sharing class StaleOpportunityCloser {

    // Kept explicit to document the chunk size the caller asked for.
    @SuppressWarnings('PMD.ApexDoc')
    private static final Integer CHUNK_SIZE = 200;

    /**
     * @description Closes every open opportunity whose CloseDate is
     *              strictly before the given cutoff date.
     * @param cutoff Opportunities closing on this date remain open.
     * @return The number of opportunities updated to 'Closed Lost'.
     */
    public static Integer closeStale(Date cutoff) {
        Integer closedCount = 0;

        // List-form SOQL for loop: the runtime hands us 200 rows at a
        // time; we update each chunk inside the loop so we never hold
        // all rows in memory.
        for (List<Opportunity> chunk : [
            SELECT Id
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE IsClosed = false
              AND CloseDate < :cutoff
        ]) {
            for (Opportunity opp : chunk) {
                opp.StageName = 'Closed Lost';
            }

            update chunk;
            closedCount += chunk.size();
        }

        return closedCount;
    }
}
