```apex
public with sharing class FollowUpTaskService {

    private static final Integer FOLLOW_UP_DAYS = 7;

    /**
     * Creates a follow-up Task for each Opportunity passed in.
     *
     * Returns the Ids of the opportunities for which no Task could be created
     * (e.g. rejected by a validation rule). Returns an empty set when every
     * Task was created successfully.
     *
     * NOTE: Implemented with a single partial-success DML (Database.insert with
     * allOrNone=false) rather than per-row inserts in a loop. See the design
     * note at the bottom of this class.
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOppIds = new Set<Id>();

        // Defensive: nothing to do for a null or empty input.
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOppIds;
        }

        // Dedupe and drop nulls so we don't create duplicate tasks or a bogus query filter.
        Set<Id> requestedIds = new Set<Id>(opportunityIds);
        requestedIds.remove(null);

        if (requestedIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOppIds;
        }

        // One query for everything we need (Name for the subject, OwnerId to
        // assign the task to the opportunity owner). Bulk-safe and only the
        // fields actually used.
        Map<Id, Opportunity> oppsById = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
             FROM Opportunity
             WHERE Id IN :requestedIds]
        );

        // Anything that was requested but does not exist (deleted record, bad Id,
        // or an Id from a different object) can never get a task, so report it
        // as a failure. Start with all requested Ids, then remove the ones we found.
        failedOppIds.addAll(requestedIds);
        failedOppIds.removeAll(oppsById.keySet());

        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        for (Opportunity opp : oppsById.values()) {
            tasks.add(new Task(
                WhatId       = opp.Id,
                OwnerId      = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject      = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(FOLLOW_UP_DAYS),
                Status       = 'Not Started',
                Priority     = 'Normal'
            ));
        }

        if (!tasks.isEmpty()) {
            // allOrNone=false: each row is saved or failed independently, exactly
            // matching the requirement that one validation-rule rejection must not
            // stop the others, while staying well inside the DML governor limits.
            List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(tasks, false);

            for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
                if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                    // Row-level failure (validation rule, trigger error, field
                    // permissions, ...). Collect the parent opportunity so the
                    // caller knows whose follow-up did not get created.
                    failedOppIds.add(tasks[i].WhatId);
                }
            }
        }

        return failedOppIds;
    }

    /*
     * DESIGN NOTE — why I did not use the literal "insert each task on its own
     * inside a try/catch" pattern:
     *
     * The review covers 150–250 opportunities passed in a single call. Synchronous
     * Apex allows at most 150 DML statements per transaction, so a per-row insert
     * loop works only until the 151st record, then the whole run dies with a
     * LimitException — which is not even catchable. The requirement itself
     * ("one rejected task must not stop the others" + report which ones failed)
     * is exactly what partial-success DML is for:
     *
     *   Database.insert(tasks, allOrNone=false)
     *
     * It performs a single DML statement, saves every row that passes the
     * validation rules, fails only the rows that don't, and hands back per-row
     * SaveResults so we can return the failed opportunities' Ids — identical
     * observable behaviour to the try/catch loop, without the governor-limit
     * ceiling. It is also faster (one round trip to the database) and is the
     * pattern Salesforce documents for "insert many records, tolerate some
     * failures".
     */
}
```

**What I did differently, and why**

- **One `Database.insert(tasks, false)` instead of an insert inside the loop.** With 150–250 opportunities the literal try/catch-per-row approach would hit the 150-DML-statement governor limit and throw an uncatchable `LimitException`, killing the whole batch — the opposite of "a rejected task must not stop the others". Partial-success insert gives the same behaviour (per-row success/failure), returns per-row errors via `Database.SaveResult`, and uses a single DML statement.
- **One SOQL query for `Name` and `OwnerId`** instead of per-record queries (per-record query would hit the 100-query limit even sooner). Only the fields actually used are selected.
- **Input hygiene:** dedupes the incoming Ids (so an opportunity listed twice gets one task, not two) and drops nulls. Opportunities that don't exist (deleted, or an Id that isn't an Opportunity) are added to the returned failure set, since no task can be created for them.
