public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {

    /**
     * For each lead, returns the Id of the first contact (in the given order)
     * whose email matches the lead's email, or null if there is no match.
     *
     * Matching rules:
     *  - Case-insensitive, ignores leading/trailing whitespace.
     *  - Blank (null or whitespace-only) emails never match.
     *  - First matching contact in `contacts` order wins.
     *
     * Purely in-memory: no SOQL, no DML, safe to call inside the import transaction.
     */
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> matchedContactIds = new List<Id>();

        // Degenerate inputs: nothing to match against / nothing to match.
        if (leads == null || leads.isEmpty()) {
            return matchedContactIds;
        }
        if (contacts == null || contacts.isEmpty()) {
            // Still return a same-size, all-null list to preserve the contract.
            for (Integer i = 0; i < leads.size(); i++) {
                matchedContactIds.add(null);
            }
            return matchedContactIds;
        }

        // Index the contacts once: normalized email -> Id of the FIRST contact
        // with that email. putIfAbsent-style check preserves contact order.
        Map<String, Id> contactIdByEmail = new Map<String, Id>();
        for (Contact contact : contacts) {
            String normalizedEmail = normalizeEmail(contact.Email);
            if (normalizedEmail != null && !contactIdByEmail.containsKey(normalizedEmail)) {
                contactIdByEmail.put(normalizedEmail, contact.Id);
            }
        }

        // One lookup per lead keeps result size and order identical to `leads`.
        for (Lead lead : leads) {
            String normalizedEmail = normalizeEmail(lead.Email);
            matchedContactIds.add(normalizedEmail == null ? null : contactIdByEmail.get(normalizedEmail));
        }

        return matchedContactIds;
    }

    /**
     * Normalizes an email for comparison: trims whitespace, lowercases.
     * Returns null for blank emails (they never participate in matching).
     */
    private static String normalizeEmail(String email) {
        if (String.isBlank(email)) {
            return null;
        }
        String trimmed = email.trim();
        if (String.isBlank(trimmed)) {
            return null;
        }
        return trimmed.toLowerCase();
    }
}
```

**On doing it differently:** you suggested a nested lead × contact loop, and honestly it's defensible at 3,000 × 3,000 (~9M iterations, a second or two of CPU). I'd still do it differently, for three reasons:

1. **CPU headroom inside the import transaction.** A naive nested loop re-runs `trim()`/`toLowerCase()` on both emails for every comparison, so the normalization cost is paid ~9M times instead of ~6K times. Building the `Map<String, Id>` once and doing one lookup per lead is O(leads + contacts) instead of O(leads × contacts), leaving plenty of CPU limit headroom for the rest of the import transaction (DML, triggers, other logic sharing the same 10s synchronous budget).
2. **The "first contact wins" rule is trivially preserved** — only put an entry in the map if the key isn't there yet, which reproduces "first in `contacts` order" exactly.
3. **Readability isn't lost.** The method is still short and flat; the map is just an index, which is arguably *more* readable than a nested loop with a labeled break.

If you'd rather keep the literal nested loop (e.g., to avoid a second collection in memory), the practical mitigation is to normalize both email lists into parallel arrays once up front, so the inner loop only does cheap `==` comparisons — that alone removes most of the CPU cost. The public behavior would be identical either way.

One edge case worth confirming with the team: leads with blank emails get `null` (never match), and duplicate contacts with the same email resolve to the earliest one — both per your spec.

**What I changed and why**

The request as written — one `System.enqueueJob` per case inside an after-update trigger — will fail in production. Apex allows a maximum of **50 `enqueueJob` calls per transaction**. Agents bulk-closing from list views and the nightly cleanup closing 200 cases would hit `System.LimitException: Too many queueable jobs added to the queue`, which rolls back the **entire** close operation — worse than the problem being solved.

**Change 1 — chunking instead of one job per case.** The trigger collects the IDs of newly closed cases with a contact, then enqueues one `CaseSurveyJob` per chunk of 50 cases. A 200-case close produces 4 jobs, comfortably inside the 50-job-per-transaction limit. Isolation is preserved where it matters: each *chunk* is an independent async transaction, so an unexpected exception in one chunk (and, once the vendor callout arrives, in one survey request) cannot affect the others.

**Change 2 — `CaseSurveyJob` takes a `List<Id>`.** Consequence of chunking. All task fields are exactly as specified (`WhatId`, `WhoId`, `'CSAT survey: ' + CaseNumber`, today + 2 days, `'Not Started'`).

**Change 3 — `Database.AllowsCallouts`.** The vendor integration is planned, and callouts from Queueable are only possible on jobs that implement this interface at compile time. Adding it now avoids a schema/test churn later. It has no effect until a callout is made.

**Change 4 — the job re-queries the cases.** The trigger passes only IDs; the job queries `CaseNumber` and `ContactId` itself. This avoids carrying stale data across the async boundary and naturally drops any case whose contact was removed between enqueue and execution (the "no contact → no task" rule is enforced both in the trigger filter and in the SOQL `WHERE ContactId != null`).

**Change 5 — `inherited sharing` and partial-insert safety.** `inherited sharing` so the job never widens data access beyond its caller. `Database.insert(tasks, false)` so one malformed record can't discard the whole chunk's tasks; if you'd rather have all-or-nothing per chunk, change it to `insert tasks`.

Note on limits: a chunk of 50 DML rows is trivially within limits. If the nightly job ever closes more than 2,500 cases (50 jobs × 50), the trigger would again hit the 50-job cap — at that point switch to chunked chaining, but that's unnecessary at the stated volumes.

